["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"aep","stream_identity":"01a0d3bf-067c-7068-af65-bd964ce78fcb","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"# Story: One artifact graph, built from several stores\n\n## Outcome\n\nOne `board` instead of three. Somebody asks a question once and gets an answer over every member,\nwith each artifact still saying which repository it came from.\n\n## Context\n\nA `MarkdownStore` answers for one repository. The assembly is what lets the verbs answer for\nseveral without any of them learning about members individually. The failure mode to design for is\npartial success: an assembly that quietly answered from two members when it was asked about three\nwould give a smaller answer that looks exactly like a complete one.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`Assembly` reads each member's store and keeps the member's name on every artifact; one index\nresolves a reference across all members; **nothing is renamed and no id is rewritten**, so reading a\nstore through the assembly and reading it alone give the same artifacts; a member that failed to\nload produces an **empty member rather than an absent one**, and `Assembly::failures` carries every\nfailure with its member's name attached; `find_cycle` holds over the combined graph.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nCaching, incremental re-read, and any persistence of the assembled graph. It is built per command.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"ent-726f6c793368125f964c1c79bc4eb2c6","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/assemble-across-sources","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":4,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"# Story: One artifact graph, built from several stores\n\n## Outcome\n\nOne `board` instead of three. Somebody asks a question once and gets an answer over every member,\nwith each artifact still saying which repository it came from.\n\n## Context\n\nA `MarkdownStore` answers for one repository. The assembly is what lets the verbs answer for\nseveral without any of them learning about members individually. The failure mode to design for is\npartial success: an assembly that quietly answered from two members when it was asked about three\nwould give a smaller answer that looks exactly like a complete one.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`Assembly` reads each member's store and keeps the member's name on every artifact; one index\nresolves a reference across all members; **nothing is renamed and no id is rewritten**, so reading a\nstore through the assembly and reading it alone give the same artifacts; a member that failed to\nload produces an **empty member rather than an absent one**, and `Assembly::failures` carries every\nfailure with its member's name attached; `find_cycle` holds over the combined graph.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nCaching, incremental re-read, and any persistence of the assembled graph. It is built per command.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:one-cli-many-repositories"},{"depends_on":"story:namespaced-identity"},{"informed_by":"entity-runtime/story:typed-references"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"Each artifact remembers which member it came from. The failure to design for is a cycle that exists only once two members are read together - find_cycle runs per relation kind today and has never seen a graph it did not fully hold.","title":"One artifact graph, built from several stores"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"},"relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:one-cli-many-repositories"},{"depends_on":"story:namespaced-identity"},{"informed_by":"entity-runtime/story:typed-references"}],"summary":"Each artifact remembers which member it came from. The failure to design for is a cycle that exists only once two members are read together - find_cycle runs per relation kind today and has neve